---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site-nav.js';
const path='/tennis-rules/';
const title='Tennis Rules Explained | Serving, Faults, Lets & How Points Are Lost';
const description='The rules of tennis explained from the ITF Rules of Tennis: serving, foot faults, lets, keeping the ball in play, changing ends and how points are lost.';
const updated='5 November 2026';
const ITF_RULES='https://www.itftennis.com/en/about-us/governance/rules-and-regulations/';
const faqs=[
  {q:'Who sets the official rules of tennis?',a:'The International Tennis Federation (ITF) publishes the Rules of Tennis, which apply worldwide. Tournaments may choose alternative formats allowed by the rules, such as No-Ad scoring or a match tie-break.'},
  {q:'Is a ball on the line in or out?',a:'In. A ball that touches any part of a line is good, because the lines are part of the court they enclose.'},
  {q:'How many serves do you get?',a:'Two. If the first serve is a fault, the server takes a second serve. Two faults in a row is a double fault and the server loses the point.'},
  {q:'Is a let on serve replayed?',a:'Under standard rules, yes: a serve that touches the net and then lands in the correct service box is a let and that serve is replayed. Some events use a "no-let" format, where the ball is played wherever it lands.'},
  {q:'Can I hit the ball before it bounces?',a:'Yes, except when receiving a serve. Hitting the ball in the air (a volley) is legal anywhere on your side of the net, but the serve must bounce before the receiver hits it.'},
];
const articleSchema={'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'Article',headline:'Tennis Rules Explained',description,inLanguage:'en-MY',datePublished:'2026-06-19',dateModified:'2026-11-05',mainEntityOfPage:new URL(path,SITE.url).href,author:{'@type':'Organization',name:`${SITE.name} Editorial Team`},publisher:{'@type':'Organization',name:SITE.name,logo:{'@type':'ImageObject',url:new URL('/logo.png',SITE.url).href}},citation:ITF_RULES};
const crumbs=[{name:'Home',href:'/'},{name:'Tennis',href:'/malaysia-tennis-events/'},{name:'Tennis Rules',href:path}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} type="article" schema={[articleSchema]} updated={updated}>
  <div class="page-band"><div class="container"><Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} /><h1>Tennis Rules Explained</h1><p class="intro">How play works: serving, faults and lets, keeping the ball in play, and the infringements that lose you a point.</p></div></div>
  <section class="section"><div class="container prose">
    <p class="lead">This guide covers <strong>how play works</strong>. For how points, games, sets and tie-breaks are counted, see <a href="/how-tennis-scoring-works/">how tennis scoring works</a>. Both guides follow the ITF <a href={ITF_RULES} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rules of Tennis</a>, the official rules used worldwide, including at Malaysian events.</p>

    <h2 id="court">The court</h2>
    <p>A tennis court is 23.77 m long. It is 8.23 m wide for singles and 10.97 m wide for doubles, which adds the “alleys” or tramlines on each side. The net is 0.914 m high at the centre. Each half has two service boxes, bounded by the service line 6.40 m from the net. Lines count as part of the court they enclose, so <strong>a ball touching a line is in</strong>.</p>

    <h2 id="serve">Serving</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The server stands <strong>behind the baseline</strong>, between the imaginary extension of the centre mark and the sideline.</li>
      <li>In each game the first point is served from the <strong>right-hand (deuce) side</strong>; the server then alternates sides every point.</li>
      <li>The serve must clear the net and land in the <strong>diagonally opposite service box</strong>.</li>
      <li>The server gets <strong>two attempts</strong>. Two faults in a row is a <strong>double fault</strong>, and the point goes to the receiver.</li>
      <li>The receiver must let the serve <strong>bounce</strong> before returning it.</li>
    </ul>
    <figure class="diagram">
      <img src="/diagrams/serve-diagonals.svg" alt="Top-down tennis court diagram drawn to scale. A server behind the baseline right of the centre mark serves diagonally into the receiver's deuce service box, shaded yellow; from left of the centre mark the serve goes into the advantage box, shaded blue." width="560" height="620" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Where the serve must land: yellow box at even scores, blue box at odd scores.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3 id="faults">What counts as a fault</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The serve lands outside the correct service box, or hits the net and doesn’t go over.</li>
      <li>The server misses the ball completely when trying to hit it.</li>
      <li><strong>Foot fault:</strong> during the service motion the server touches the baseline or the court inside it with either foot, walks or runs, or steps beyond the imaginary extension of the sideline or centre mark.</li>
      <li>The served ball hits a permanent fixture, singles stick or net post before it bounces, or touches the server or the server’s partner.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Tossing the ball up and catching it instead of hitting it is <strong>not</strong> a fault; the server simply tosses again.</p>

    <h3 id="let">Lets</h3>
    <p>A <strong>let on serve</strong> happens when the serve touches the net, strap or band and then lands in the correct box, or touches the net and then the receiver or their partner before bouncing. The serve is replayed and the earlier fault, if any, still counts. A let is also called when play is interrupted, for example by a ball rolling onto the court; the whole point is replayed. Some events use a <strong>No-Let</strong> rule, an approved alternative under the ITF rules, where a net-cord serve is played as it lands. ITF junior events use No-Let in all matches in 2026.</p>

    <h2 id="in-play">Keeping the ball in play</h2>
    <p>After the serve, players may hit the ball before or after one bounce, from anywhere on their side of the net. A rally continues until one side cannot make a good return. The ball may touch the net during a rally and still be good.</p>

    <h2 id="lose-point">Ways to lose a point</h2>
    <p>Under the ITF rules you lose the point if, while the ball is in play:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the ball bounces <strong>twice</strong> on your side before you hit it;</li>
      <li>you hit the ball <strong>out</strong> or into the net;</li>
      <li>you, your racket or your clothing <strong>touch the net</strong>, net posts or your opponent’s court;</li>
      <li>you hit the ball <strong>before it has crossed the net</strong> to your side;</li>
      <li>the ball <strong>touches you</strong> or anything you are wearing or carrying, other than your racket;</li>
      <li>you deliberately <strong>carry or catch</strong> the ball on the racket, or deliberately hit it twice;</li>
      <li>you <strong>throw your racket</strong> and it hits the ball;</li>
      <li>you deliberately <strong>hinder</strong> your opponent. An unintentional hindrance, such as a dropped ball, means the point is replayed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="ends">Changing ends</h2>
    <p>Players change ends after the <strong>first, third and every following odd game</strong> of each set. Within a tie-break they change after every six points. A short rest of up to 90 seconds is allowed at changes of ends, except after the first game of a set and during tie-breaks, when players change without a rest.</p>

    <h2 id="doubles">Doubles order</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Each team decides who serves first in each set, and the server rotates between the four players: team A player 1, team B player 1, team A player 2, team B player 2.</li>
      <li>Each team chooses who receives in the deuce court and who in the advantage court, and keeps that order for the whole set.</li>
      <li>Partners can stand anywhere on their side; only the server and receiver have fixed positions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="officials">Officials and line calls</h2>
    <p>In matches without umpires, common at junior and club level in Malaysia, each player calls the lines on their own side. Calls should be prompt, and a doubtful ball is given to the opponent. Where there is a chair umpire, their decision on facts is final. Some events add a roving official or a referee to resolve disputes.</p>

    <h2 id="etiquette">Court etiquette</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Wait until a point ends before walking behind another court.</li>
      <li>Announce the score before serving, especially in unofficiated matches.</li>
      <li>Return balls from other courts between points.</li>
      <li>Shake hands at the end of the match.</li>
    </ul>

    <Faq items={faqs} />
    <div class="related callout info"><strong>Related:</strong> <a href="/how-tennis-scoring-works/">How scoring works: deuce, tie-breaks and match formats</a> · <a href="/malaysia-tennis-events/">Choosing your first tournament</a> · <a href="/tennis-courts-malaysia/">Where to play in Malaysia</a></div>
    <p class="note" style="font-size:14px;color:var(--slate)">Source: ITF <a href={ITF_RULES} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rules of Tennis</a>. This is a plain-language summary; for officiated play the full ITF rules and the tournament’s own regulations apply.</p>
  </div></section>
  <style>
    .diagram{margin:18px auto 22px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;padding:12px;background:#fff;max-width:600px}
    .diagram img{width:100%;height:auto}
    .diagram figcaption{font-size:13px;color:var(--slate);margin-top:6px}
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
